        "text": "C.1 DIFFERENTIALLY-EXPRESSED GENES\nIn order to evaluate the quality of the predictions on the genes that were substantially affected by the perturbations, we select sets of 50 differentially-expressed genes associated with each perturbation and separately evaluate model performance on these genes. This is the same procedure carried out by Lotfollahi et al. (2021a).\nC.2 OUT-OF-DISTRIBUTION SELECTIONS\nWe randomly select a covariate category (e.g. a cell type) and hold out all cells in this category that received one of the twenty perturbations whose effects are the hardest to predict. We use these held-out data to compose the out-of-distribution (OOD) set. We computed the euclidean distance between the pseudobulked gene expression of each perturbation against the rest of the dataset, and selected the top twenty most distant ones as the hardest-to-predict perturbations. This is the same procedure carried out by Lotfollahi et al. (2021a).\nC.3 TRAINING\nThe data excluding the OOD set are split into training and validation set with a four-to-one ratio. All common hyperparameters of all models (network width, network depth, learning rate, decay rate, etc.) are set to the same as Lotfollahi et al. (2021a)."
